Run QtDBus on the thread that opens the connection, not on a manager thread.

Every guest thread is a Web Worker on this wasm port, and the host compiles
the whole program module a second time for the first one a process starts
(docs/browser-support.md#firefox-executable-code-limit) — on Firefox that
second copy of a Qt client does not fit SpiderMonkey's 2 GiB code region.
QDBusConnectionManager is a QDaemonThread that owns every connection and
answers the application through BlockingQueuedConnection calls, so opening
the session bus started a thread. On wasm the manager never starts: the
connections live on the calling thread, the cross-thread calls become
direct calls, the destroyed-object hooks use the same-thread connection
type, and a blocking wait on a pending call lets libdbus read the reply
(the connection's socket notifier cannot run while this thread blocks).
Everything else — watches, timeouts, dispatch, object activation — already
runs through the thread the connection lives in.

